{"3":{"inputs":{"cfg":["23",0],"seed":["20",0],"model":["46",0],"steps":["22",0],"denoise":1,"negative":["7",0],"positive":["6",0],"scheduler":"karras","latent_image":["97",0],"sampler_name":"dpmpp_2m"},"class_type":"KSampler"},"4":{"inputs":{"ckpt_name":["50",0]},"class_type":"CheckpointLoaderSimple"},"6":{"inputs":{"clip":["46",1],"text":["47",0]},"class_type":"CLIPTextEncode"},"7":{"inputs":{"clip":["46",1],"text":["80",0]},"class_type":"CLIPTextEncode"},"8":{"inputs":{"vae":["4",2],"samples":["3",0]},"class_type":"VAEDecode"},"9":{"inputs":{"images":["8",0],"filename_prefix":["87",0]},"class_type":"SaveImage"},"20":{"inputs":{"input_id":"input_seed","description":"The seed value is passed into the KSampler node and determines the random number initialization used during the denoising process. Changing the seed alters the specific noise pattern used in generation, which causes the model to produce different interpretations of the same prompt and parameters. When held constant, it enables deterministic (reproducible) outputs. When randomized, it produces new variations. The seed does not alter the overall structure of the prompt or style but modifies the composition, pose, and details within the model’s learned distribution. Low values or specific seeds may also bias toward known cached outputs from training data. This parameter is vital for exploring prompt space while maintaining control over randomness.","display_name":"","default_value":88888888},"class_type":"ComfyUIDeployExternalNumberInt"},"22":{"inputs":{"input_id":"input_steps","description":"This controls the number of sampling steps performed in the KSampler. It defines how many iterative refinement cycles the model will perform to denoise the latent image. More steps allow the model to refine the output more thoroughly, often producing sharper and more coherent results, but at the cost of generation time. Fewer steps result in faster images but can lead to incomplete or artifact-ridden compositions. The effect is global and applies uniformly across all conditioning, LoRAs, and prompt information. The ideal value depends on the chosen sampler (e.g., DPM++ 2M with Karras) and the complexity of the prompt. For high-fidelity or multi-LoRA scenarios, slightly higher step counts are recommended to allow all conditioning signals to fully express themselves.","display_name":"","default_value":30},"class_type":"ComfyUIDeployExternalNumberInt"},"23":{"inputs":{"input_id":"input_cfg","description":"This is the classifier-free guidance scale, a floating-point number passed into the KSampler. It adjusts the strength with which the model is guided by the text embeddings from the positive prompt. A low CFG value results in more creative, freeform, or unexpected outputs; a high CFG value forces the model to rigidly adhere to the prompt at the cost of creative variance. When multiple LoRAs are active or when the prompt includes subtle or abstract details, a moderate CFG (6–8) typically yields a balance between fidelity and interpretability. Extremely high CFGs (12+) can lead to over-sharpening, repetitive noise, or collapsed output. This parameter is one of the most sensitive controls for tuning how literally or liberally the prompt is followed.","display_name":"","default_value":7},"class_type":"ComfyUIDeployExternalNumber"},"46":{"inputs":{"clip":["4",1],"text":["47",1],"model":["4",0]},"class_type":"MultiLoraLoader-70bf3d77"},"47":{"inputs":{"text":["48",0]},"class_type":"LoraTextExtractor-b1f83aa2"},"48":{"inputs":{"input_id":"input_prompt","description":"This is the primary textual prompt that defines the desired content of the image. It is routed into a preprocessing system that performs automatic detection and transformation of trigger words linked to specific LoRAs. When a user includes a known stylistic, character-based, or thematic keyword—such as a LoRA trigger like “kemonokaki”—the backend will rewrite the prompt to include the correct ComfyUI LoRA syntax in-line, such as <lora:kemonokaki:1.0>. This transformation supports weight overrides, so phrases like “kemonokaki:0.4” will result in <lora:kemonokaki:0.4> syntax being injected instead. After rewriting, the prompt passes through the LoraTextExtractor node, which separates LoRA markup from the raw prompt text. The cleaned prompt is then fed to CLIPTextEncode, where it is embedded into a conditioning tensor. That conditioning directly influences the final image by guiding the model during sampling, biasing it toward the concepts and aesthetics described in the prompt. The input_prompt therefore exerts a dominant influence over both subject and style of the final output and is the most semantically rich user input.","display_name":"","default_value":""},"class_type":"ComfyUIDeployExternalText"},"50":{"inputs":{"input_id":"input_checkpoint","description":"This input controls the base model used in generation. It is passed to CheckpointLoaderSimple, which loads the specified SDXL .safetensors file and returns a model, a CLIP encoder, and a VAE. These are then distributed downstream to several nodes: the model is sent to the MultiLoraLoader and KSampler, the CLIP to the MultiLoraLoader and CLIPTextEncode, and the VAE to the VAEDecode node. The checkpoint selection determines the foundational model weights, including its stylistic tendencies, resolution handling, and semantic vocabulary. Switching checkpoints can dramatically alter the look and coherence of the image, even with the same prompt. Additionally, compatibility between LoRAs and the base checkpoint is crucial—using LoRAs trained on a different model may produce unexpected or degraded results. This input is often tuned less frequently than others but is critical for setting the baseline behavior of the entire workflow.","display_name":"","default_value":"zavychromaxl_v60.safetensors"},"class_type":"ComfyUIDeployExternalCheckpoint"},"78":{"inputs":{"input_id":"input_batch","description":"This parameter controls the batch size, passed into the EmptyLatentImage node. It determines how many images are generated in parallel. The effect is mostly operational—higher batch sizes produce multiple outputs in one call, each with different noise sampled from the same seed context. All images share the same prompt and parameters. While batch size does not directly affect visual content, it can be useful for producing variations efficiently or for visual comparison between multiple results under the same conditions. In constrained systems, high batch sizes may lead to out-of-memory errors.","display_name":"","default_value":1},"class_type":"ComfyUIDeployExternalNumberInt"},"80":{"inputs":{"input_id":"input_negative","description":"This is the negative prompt field. It functions as a suppressor or subtractive influence during generation by discouraging the model from including features or styles specified here. The input is passed through CLIPTextEncode to produce negative conditioning, which is then routed into the KSampler as the negative guidance tensor. This signal is applied throughout the denoising process to repel the model away from certain visual elements, behaviors, or features. The effect of this input is subtractive and is most noticeable when the user needs to avoid artifacts, unwanted subjects, stylistic drift, or inconsistencies. Typical values might include embeddings like “easynegative,” or descriptive exclusions like “blurry, low quality, disfigured.” The balance between negative and positive prompts, modulated further by CFG, shapes the model’s adherence to desirable traits versus its avoidance of undesired ones.","display_name":"","default_value":"embedding:easynegative, "},"class_type":"ComfyUIDeployExternalText"},"87":{"inputs":{"input_id":"input_text","description":"This is a metadata parameter passed into the SaveImage node. It sets the prefix of the filename used when saving the generated image to disk. This has no effect on the generation itself, but it is useful for organizing results, associating outputs with prompts, or tagging them with user/session-specific identifiers. When used programmatically, it allows systems to trace outputs to their source workflows or user inputs. Though minor in influence, it can be important for managing multi-user or automated runs.","display_name":"","default_value":"stationthisbotquickmake"},"class_type":"ComfyUIDeployExternalText"},"97":{"inputs":{"width":["99",0],"height":["98",0],"batch_size":["78",0]},"class_type":"EmptyLatentImage"},"98":{"inputs":{"input_id":"input_height","description":"","display_name":"","default_value":1024},"class_type":"ComfyUIDeployExternalNumberInt"},"99":{"inputs":{"input_id":"input_width","description":"","display_name":"","default_value":1024},"class_type":"ComfyUIDeployExternalNumberInt"}}
